# amiga-a3000 - a Commodore Amiga 3000: a 25 MHz 68030 with its 68882, the
# Enhanced Chip Set, 2 MiB of chip RAM, and a WD33C93A SCSI port behind
# Commodore's own DMA controller.
#
#   rsemu run amiga-a3000 --media kickstart=kickstart:amiga-os-310-a3000.rom \
#       --media hd0=workbench-311.hdf
#   rsemu run amiga-a3000 --media kickstart=kick.rom --drive hd0=disk.hdf
#   rsemu run amiga-a3000 --media kickstart=kick.rom --media df0=disk.adf --vnc :5900
#
# `hd0` is a whole disk: an Amiga hard-disk image with a Rigid Disk Block in
# its first blocks (an HDF that begins `RDSK`), which is what Kickstart's
# `scsi.device` looks for. The very same image the A600 boots from its IDE
# port - the RDB is a partition table, not a cable.
#
# What changes from `amiga-a500plus.machine`, and where each change comes from:
#
# * **A 68030 and a 68882.** "MC68030 32-bit microprocessor ... MC68882
#   floating point coprocessor" running at 25 MHz on the A3000-25 (A3000
#   Technical Reference, system overview). The processor is its own oscillator
#   tree: it does not divide the chipset crystal the way a 68000 does on an
#   A500, which is the whole reason the machine is faster than its chips.
# * **A 32-bit address space**, because the processor has 32 address pins.
#   Everything the chip set answers is still in the first 16 MiB, which is
#   what keeps `$00DFF000` at `$00DFF000`; the space is wider so that a Zorro
#   III address is an address rather than a wrap.
# * **2 MiB of chip RAM at $000000.** The A3000's Agnus is the 2 MiB ECS part
#   and the board fills it. Motherboard *fast* RAM is Ramsey's, top-aligned at
#   `$08000000` - see the note below.
# * **The Enhanced Chip Set**: an 8372B Agnus and an 8373 Super Denise, the
#   same parts `amiga-a500plus` has. `graphics.library` reports `$03` in
#   `GfxBase->ChipRevBits0` for them, which `tests/amiga_a3000.rs` asserts.
# * **SCSI in place of nothing.** An A500+ has no hard disk at all and an A600
#   has IDE behind Gayle. The A3000 has a **WD33C93A** and Commodore's **DMAC**,
#   and the SCSI chip's two registers are mapped *inside* the DMA controller's
#   register space (*The A3000+ System Specification* section 2.4.1), so one
#   window at `$00DD0000` covers both parts. See `src/dev/amiga/sdmac.rs` for
#   the register map and `src/dev/wd33c93.rs` for the controller.
# * **A battery-backed clock** on the motherboard at `$00DC0000`, as on an
#   A500 with an A501 - the A3000 has one soldered on.
#
# Everything else - the crystal, the CIAs and their decode, Paula, Agnus,
# Denise, DF0, the keyboard and the mouse - is `amiga-a500plus.machine`'s, and
# that file carries the long comments for it.
#
# ## The ROM, and the "ROM tower"
#
# This board maps a 512 KiB Kickstart at `$00F80000` and nothing else, which is
# what an A3000 with a real Kickstart in its sockets is. Two things are worth
# writing down because they are easy to get wrong:
#
# * The earliest A3000s shipped with a **bootstrap ROM** - Kickstart 1.4 or
#   2.0 beta, "SuperKickstart" - which is not an operating system. It finds a
#   Kickstart *partition* on the SCSI disk, copies the real Kickstart into RAM
#   at `$00F80000` and jumps to it. `amiga-os-140-a3000.rom` is such an image.
#   Booting one needs an HDF with that partition on it, which the Amiga Forever
#   Workbench images do not have, so this board is not tested with it. A
#   shipped Kickstart - 2.04 (37.175) or 3.1 (40.68), both 512 KiB - needs
#   none of that machinery and is what the tests use.
# * There is therefore **no ROM tower here**: no second ROM socket, no
#   `$00E00000` extended-ROM window (that is the A500 board's, and only AROS
#   fills it), and no write-enabled RAM shadow of `$00F80000`. A machine file
#   for a bootstrap-ROM A3000 would need the last of those three.
#
# ## Motherboard fast RAM
#
# An A3000 has 1 to 16 MiB of 32-bit motherboard RAM behind Ramsey, and this
# board fits 4 MiB of it by default (`-p fast-ram=`). Section 2.2 of the
# A3000+ System Specification places it "from $07FFFFFF building down toward
# $07000000, as far as the populated memory will take it", so the `map`
# statement below is top-aligned at `$08000000` rather than based at
# `$07000000` - a half-populated board leaves the *bottom* of the window empty,
# not the top. Kickstart finds it, relocates `ExecBase` into it and boots.
#
# This window is why the board needs `amiga.ramsey` at all: Kickstart's memory
# sizing spins on `$00DE0003` reading back what it wrote before it will size
# anything here.
#
# Zorro II autoconfig space at `$00E80000`, the unfitted bottom of the fast RAM
# window and Zorro III space above `$08000000` all float: no cards.
#
# Written from the *Amiga Hardware Reference Manual* 3rd edition (Appendix D,
# the memory map), *The A3000+ System Specification* (section 2.4, the DMAC and
# its register table), the *WD33C93A SCSI Bus Interface Controller* data sheet
# (November 1990) and X3.131-1994 for the bus. No Amiga emulator source, no
# AROS source and no Kickstart disassembly was consulted (ROADMAP.md 1).
#
# Empty addresses float rather than fault, for the reasons the A500 file gives:
# Gary generates /DTACK for every range no other chip claims.

machine "amiga-a3000" {
  # -- how much of each memory -----------------------------------------------
  param chip-ram = 2M

  # How much 32-bit motherboard RAM is in Ramsey's sockets, 1 to 16 MiB. "The
  # RAMSEY chip directly manages 16MB of 32 bit wide Fast RAM ... RAMSEY
  # controlled Fast RAM is located from $07FFFFFF building down toward
  # $07000000, as far as the populated memory will take it" (The A3000+ System
  # Specification section 2.2), so the window is **top-aligned** at $08000000
  # and a smaller fitting starts higher up, which is what the `map` below
  # computes. `-p fast-ram=16M` is a fully populated board.
  #
  # There is no way to say "no SIMMs at all" here, because a `ram` object
  # cannot have no bytes; a board with none is the A500's story and not worth a
  # second machine file. 4 MiB is four banks of 256Kx4 parts, which is what
  # RAMSIZE low means (section 2.2.1).
  param fast-ram = 4M

  # Every Kickstart that runs on a 3000 is 512 KiB.
  param kickstart-size = 512K

  # Whether Paula keeps stereo output frames for a host audio sink to drain.
  param audio = false

  # The date the battery-backed clock starts at. Never the host's: a device
  # may not read the host clock (CLAUDE.md, determinism), so it is declared,
  # and two runs of this file see the same day.
  param time = "2026-01-01T00:00:00"

  # -- the crystals ----------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # Two trees, because the A3000 has two. The chip set runs off the 28.37516
  # MHz PAL crystal exactly as an A500's does; the processor runs off its own
  # 50 MHz oscillator divided by two. `-p master=28636360` gives the NTSC
  # chipset, which also needs `standard = "ntsc"` on `agnus` and `denise`;
  # `-p cpu-clock=16666667` gives the A3000-16.
  param master = 28375160
  param cpu-clock = 25000000

  # Which 68030: `-p processor=68ec030` is the part with no memory management
  # unit, which Commodore fitted to the A3000's cost-reduced descendants. The
  # A3000's own is the full one.
  param processor = "68030"
  osc   clk    = master Hz
  osc   cpuclk = cpu-clock Hz

  space mem { width = 32, endian = "big", unassigned = open-bus }

  # -- the processor ---------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # A 68030 with its paged memory management unit and a 68882 in the
  # coprocessor socket. `AttnFlags` in `ExecBase` is what the guest concludes
  # from this: `tests/amiga_a3000.rs` reads it back and asserts the 68030 and
  # 68882 bits are set.
  object cpu "cpu.m68k" {
    clock  = cpuclk
    space  = mem
    engine = "interp"
    model  = processor
    fpu    = "68882"
  }

  object chipram "ram" { size = chip-ram }
  object fastram "ram" { size = fast-ram }
  object kick    "rom" { size = kickstart-size, image = "kickstart" }

  object custom "amiga.custom" { }
  object paula "amiga.paula" { clock = clk / 8, custom = custom, port = "serial", record = audio }
  object df0 "amiga.floppy" { clock = clk / 8, paula = paula, image = "df0" }

  # -- Agnus: an 8372B, and Denise: an 8373 ----------------------------------
  object agnus "amiga.agnus" {
    clock    = clk / 8
    custom   = custom
    ram      = chipram
    standard = "pal"
    revision = "ecs"
    reach    = 2M
    paula    = paula
    video    = denise
  }
  object denise "amiga.denise" { custom = custom, clock = clk / 4, revision = "ecs" }

  # -- the battery-backed clock ----------------------------------------------
  osc rtcclk = 32768 Hz
  object rtc "amiga.rtc" { clock = rtcclk, time = time }

  # -- Ramsey ----------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # The memory controller, as software can see it: two registers, and the
  # reason the board needs them is that Kickstart's memory-sizing routine
  # writes the three mode bits of `$00DE0003` and *spins until they read back*.
  # `version = 13` is `$0D`, "original A3000 versions of RAMSEY"
  # (A3000+ System Specification section 2.2.2). See src/dev/amiga/ramsey.rs.
  object ramsey "amiga.ramsey" { version = 13 }

  object cia_a "mos.8520" { clock = clk / 40 }
  object cia_b "mos.8520" { clock = clk / 40 }
  object cia_a_decode "amiga.cia-decode" { chip = cia_a, lane = "odd"  }
  object cia_b_decode "amiga.cia-decode" { chip = cia_b, lane = "even" }

  # -- the SCSI port ---------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # Three objects, because the board has three things: a disk, a controller and
  # a DMA path. They meet on a named bus (`scsi0`) the way an `ata.disk` and
  # its adapter meet in a named drive bay.
  #
  # The drive is declared *first*, for the reason `pc-at.machine` gives:
  # `Machine::reset` runs devices in declaration order, and a controller's
  # reset pulls `RST` on the cable, which the targets must already be there to
  # hear. No bytes bound to `hd0` is an empty cable position - an A3000 with
  # its drive taken out, which is a machine that boots to the insert-disk
  # screen.
  #
  # `id = 0` is where Commodore's own drives shipped; `scsi.device` scans every
  # address, so any of the eight would be found.
  object hd0 "scsi.disk" {
    image    = "hd0"
    bus      = "scsi0"
    id       = 0
    vendor   = "RSEMU"
    product  = "SCSI HARDDISK"
    revision = "1.0"
  }

  # The controller. `id = 7` is the initiator's own address, which is what
  # Commodore straps and what `scsi.device` writes into the Own ID register
  # before it issues a Reset command.
  object wd0 "wd.33c93" { bus = "scsi0", id = 7 }

  # The DMA controller, and the window both chips are behind.
  object sdmac "amiga.sdmac" { scsi = wd0, space = mem }

  # -- the overlay -----------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # Gary's overlay decoder, used for exactly that: the ROM at zero while `OVL`
  # is high, chip RAM after. CIA-A's PA0 drives it, as on an A500. No `custom`
  # (bank 6 does not repeat the registers on this board), no `slow-ram`, no
  # `ext`.
  object overlay "amiga.gary" {
    space = mem
    rom   = kick
    ram   = chipram
    size  = chip-ram
  }

  # -- the memory map --------------------------------------------------------
  #
  #   $00000000  chip RAM, behind the overlay                   2 MiB
  #   $00BFD000  CIA-B, even byte lane
  #   $00BFE000  CIA-A, odd byte lane
  #   $00DC0000  the battery-backed clock                       64 KiB
  #   $00DD0000  the Super DMAC, and the WD33C93A behind it     4 KiB
  #   $00DE0000  Ramsey: the control and version registers      256 bytes
  #   $00DFF000  the custom chip registers                      512 bytes
  #   $00F80000  Kickstart                                      512 KiB
  #   $08000000-fast-ram  motherboard fast RAM, behind Ramsey
  #
  # Everything else floats: Zorro II space, autoconfig at $00E80000, the unfitted
  # part of the fast RAM window below $08000000-fast-ram, and Zorro III space
  # above $08000000.
  map mem 0x00000000 size chip-ram = overlay.overlay { endian = "big" }
  map mem 0x00BFD000 size 0x1000   = cia_b_decode    { endian = "big" }
  map mem 0x00BFE000 size 0x1000   = cia_a_decode    { endian = "big" }
  map mem 0x00DC0000 size 0x10000  = mirror(rtc)     { endian = "big" }
  map mem 0x00DD0000 size 0x1000   = sdmac.regs      { endian = "big" }
  map mem 0x00DE0000 size 0x100    = ramsey.regs     { endian = "big" }
  map mem 0x00DFF000 size 0x200    = custom
  map mem 0x00F80000 size 512K     = mirror(kick)    { endian = "big" }
  map mem 0x08000000 - fast-ram size fast-ram = fastram { endian = "big" }

  # -- wiring ----------------------------------------------------------------
  wire cia_a.pa0 -> overlay.ovl

  wire paula.ipl0 -> cpu.ipl0
  wire paula.ipl1 -> cpu.ipl1
  wire paula.ipl2 -> cpu.ipl2

  # INT2 is an open-collector net: CIA-A pulls it, and so does the SCSI port -
  # the level Paula reports as PORTS, which is where `scsi.device` puts its
  # handler. INT6 is CIA-B's, as everywhere.
  wire cia_a.irq  -> paula.int2
  wire sdmac.int  -> paula.int2
  wire cia_b.irq  -> paula.int6

  # The floppy lines, exactly as on the A500 (Appendix E; chapter 8, Table 8-5).
  wire cia_b.pb7 -> df0.mtr   { pull = "up" }    # MTR*
  wire cia_b.pb3 -> df0.sel   { pull = "up" }    # SEL0*
  wire cia_b.pb2 -> df0.side  { pull = "up" }    # SIDE*
  wire cia_b.pb1 -> df0.dir   { pull = "up" }    # DIR
  wire cia_b.pb0 -> df0.step  { pull = "up" }    # STEP*
  wire df0.rdy   -> cia_a.pa5 { pull = "up" }    # RDY*
  wire df0.tk0   -> cia_a.pa4 { pull = "up" }    # TK0*
  wire df0.wpro  -> cia_a.pa3 { pull = "up" }    # WPRO*
  wire df0.chng  -> cia_a.pa2 { pull = "up" }    # CHNG*
  wire df0.index -> cia_b.flag { pull = "up" }   # INDEX*

  wire agnus.vsync -> cia_a.tod
  wire agnus.hsync -> cia_b.tod

  # -- the keyboard and the mouse --------------------------------------------
  osc periph = 1000000 Hz

  object kbd "amiga.keyboard" { clock = periph }
  wire kbd.kclk  -> cia_a.cnt { pull = "up" }
  wire cia_a.sp  -> kbd.kdat  { pull = "up" }
  wire kbd.kdat  -> cia_a.sp

  object mouse "amiga.mouse" { clock = periph }
  wire mouse.y      -> denise.m0v  { pull = "up" }
  wire mouse.yq     -> denise.m0vq { pull = "up" }
  wire mouse.x      -> denise.m0h  { pull = "up" }
  wire mouse.xq     -> denise.m0hq { pull = "up" }
  wire mouse.left   -> cia_a.pa6   { pull = "up" }
  wire mouse.right  -> paula.potly { pull = "up" }
  wire mouse.middle -> paula.potlx { pull = "up" }
}
